## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well:
the frame loop and the delta time it hands each update, fitting the fixed logical
stage onto the canvas, the keyboard and pointer input the player drives the game
with, the audio, loading the assets you produce, and the on-screen debug
overlay. The mine
is larger than the stage, so the camera that scrolls the view over it is yours
too. The specification in `specs/` states what Deepcore needs from that layer and
fixes nothing else about it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the game
and does not restate the engine. The documentation also carries a complete worked
example of unit-testing a game built on it.

Build the game on the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame
loop and the delta time it hands your update, the input actions, the audio, asset
loading, and the on-screen debug overlay. It also holds the game's state, by
value: each frame it hands `update` the current state read-only and keeps what
`update` returns as the next, so a frame builds the next state from the current
one rather than writing into it. The engine draws nothing and holds no camera, so
the mine, the miner, and the scroll over them are yours. Register what the game
needs with the engine and use what it gives back.
{{else}}
